  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sumitomo Labour Supply Chain Mapping</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project aims to contribute to promoting ethical recruitment principles including no recruitment fees and costs to migrant workers (The Employer Pays Principle) in Sumitomo&#x2019;s labour supply chain  and will generate preliminary evidence and address knowledge gaps on recruitment and employment of Ukrainian, Kazakh and Mongolian migrant workers at Sumitomo&#x2019;s site in Hungary. Sumitomo Electric Group (Sumitomo) has committed to the Employer Pays Principle. In pursuit of successful implementation of the principle and better understanding of the risks in the labour supply chain, IOM is to conduct a labour supply chain mapping for Sumitomo. Conducting a labour supply chain mapping will increase transparency and assess risks of labour exploitation with Sumitomo labour suppliers in Ukraine, Kazakhstan, Mongolia and Hungary and identify improvement measures, where necessary. The assessment will also help assess gaps between the operations, internal policies, relevant regulatory frameworks and the IRIS Standard on ethical recruitment.  The assessment will look at the recruitment and selection process, recruitment fees and related costs, transparency of hiring, working and living conditions, and access to effective grievance mechanisms for migrant workers from Ukraine, Kazakhstan and Mongolia working at the Sumitomo site in M&#xf3;r, Hungary.</narrative>
  </description>
